<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Graph-Regularized Learning of Gaussian Mixture Models 
<br class="ltx_break">
<span class="ltx_pubnotes"><span class="ltx_pubnotes_content"><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_thanks"><span class="ltx_note_name">Thanks: </span>Funded by the Research Council of Finland (Decision #363624),
Jane and Aatos Erkko Foundation (Decision #A835),
Business Finland</span></span></span></h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">1<sup id="id1" class="ltx_sup">st</sup> Shamsiiat Abdurakhmanova
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">dept. of Computer Science</span>
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="id3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Aalto University 
<br class="ltx_break"></span>Helsinki, Finland 
<br class="ltx_break">shamsiiat.abdurakhmanova@aalto.fi
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">2<sup id="id4" class="ltx_sup">nd</sup> Alex Jung
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">dept. of Computer Science</span>
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="id6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Aalto University 
<br class="ltx_break"></span>Helsinki, Finland 
<br class="ltx_break">alex.jung@aalto.fi
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We present a graph-regularized learning of Gaussian Mixture Models (GMMs) in distributed settings with heterogeneous and limited local data. The method exploits a provided similarity graph to guide parameter sharing among nodes, avoiding the transfer of raw data. The resulting model allows for flexible aggregation of neighbors’ parameters and outperforms both centralized and locally trained GMMs in heterogeneous, low-sample regimes.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Index Terms: </h6>
Gaussian Mixture Models, Federated Learning, Graph Regularization, Expectation-Maximization, Distributed Learning

</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Introduction</span></h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose GraphFed-EM, a federated Gaussian Mixture Model in which local nodes collaboratively learn a personalized probabilistic model through graph-based regularization, without exchanging raw data. The algorithm is adapted for decentralized settings, incorporating an aggregation step that promotes parameter similarity among connected nodes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">GraphFed-EM is beneficial for heterogeneous collections of local datasets, where a single global GMM may fail due to dataset dissimilarity and local training is challenging in low-sample regimes (small sample sizes or high-dimensional features). We illustrate this with synthetic clustered datasets, where datasets within each cluster share common GMM parameters but differ across clusters. We also test our algorithm on synthetic and MNIST datasets with skewed feature distributions. Unlike prior federated EM approaches that assume identical GMM parameters across clients, GraphFed-EM flexibly aggregates parameters via a similarity graph while respecting client-specific variations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The remainder of the paper is organized as follows: Section <a href="#S2" title="II Preliminaries on Finite Gaussian Mixtures ‣ Graph-Regularized Learning of Gaussian Mixture Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a> reviews Gaussian Mixture Models and EM; Section <a href="#S3" title="III Graph-Regularized EM Algorithm ‣ Graph-Regularized Learning of Gaussian Mixture Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a> presents GraphFed-EM; Section <a href="#S4" title="IV Related Work ‣ Graph-Regularized Learning of Gaussian Mixture Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a> discusses related work; Section <a href="#S5" title="V Numerical Experiments ‣ Graph-Regularized Learning of Gaussian Mixture Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V</span></a> reports experiments; Section <a href="#S6" title="VI Graph-based aggregation as regularized EM ‣ Graph-Regularized Learning of Gaussian Mixture Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI</span></a> presents an interpretation of GraphFed-EM as regularized EM; Section <a href="#S7" title="VII Conclusions ‣ Graph-Regularized Learning of Gaussian Mixture Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VII</span></a> concludes.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Preliminaries on Finite Gaussian Mixtures</span></h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Gaussian mixture distribution can be written as a weighted sum of <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> Gaussians <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p(\mathbf{x})=\sum_{k=1}^{K}\pi_{k}\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{x}\mid\bm{\mu}_{k},\bm{\Sigma}_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p(\mathbf{x})=\sum_{k=1}^{K}\pi_{k}\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{x}\mid\bm{\mu}_{k},\bm{\Sigma}_{k})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is the mixture weight for component <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mu}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mu}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> are the mean vector and covariance matrix of component <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{x}\mid\bm{\mu}_{k},\bm{\Sigma}_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{x}\mid\bm{\mu}_{k},\bm{\Sigma}_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> is the multivariate Gaussian probability density function.
Given a set of i.i.d. observations <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}=(\mathbf{x}_{1}^{T},\dots,\mathbf{x}_{N}^{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>N</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}=(\mathbf{x}_{1}^{T},\dots,\mathbf{x}_{N}^{T})</annotation></semantics></math>, the data can be modeled using a mixture of Gaussians, with log-likelihood</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{ln}p(\mathbf{X}\mid\bm{\pi},\bm{\mu},\bm{\Sigma})=\sum_{n=1}^{N}\text{ln}\left\{\sum_{k=1}^{K}\pi_{k}\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{x}\mid\bm{\mu}_{k},\bm{\Sigma}_{k})\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>ln</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>𝝅</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝚺</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mtext>ln</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{ln}p(\mathbf{X}\mid\bm{\pi},\bm{\mu},\bm{\Sigma})=\sum_{n=1}^{N}\text{ln}\left\{\sum_{k=1}^{K}\pi_{k}\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{x}\mid\bm{\mu}_{k},\bm{\Sigma}_{k})\right\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The most commonly used method to maximize this function is the expectation-maximization (EM)
algorithm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. Briefly, the E step of the algorithm consists of computing responsibilities <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{kn}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{kn}</annotation></semantics></math> for each data point <math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and component <math id="S2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\gamma_{kn}=\frac{\pi_{k}\,\mathcal{N}\left(\mathbf{x}_{n}\mid\bm{\mu}_{k},\bm{\Sigma}_{k}\right)}{\sum_{j=1}^{K}\pi_{j}\,\mathcal{N}\left(\mathbf{x}_{n}\mid\bm{\mu}_{j},\bm{\Sigma}_{j}\right)}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\gamma_{kn}=\frac{\pi_{k}\,\mathcal{N}\left(\mathbf{x}_{n}\mid\bm{\mu}_{k},\bm{\Sigma}_{k}\right)}{\sum_{j=1}^{K}\pi_{j}\,\mathcal{N}\left(\mathbf{x}_{n}\mid\bm{\mu}_{j},\bm{\Sigma}_{j}\right)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">and M step consists of parameters update step according to closed-form solutions:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pi_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi_{k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{N_{k}}{N},\quad\text{with }N_{k}=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\gamma_{nk}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>with </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{N_{k}}{N},\quad\text{with }N_{k}=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\gamma_{nk}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{\mu}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{\mu}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{N_{k}}\sum_{n=1}^{N}\gamma_{nk}\mathbf{x}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{N_{k}}\sum_{n=1}^{N}\gamma_{nk}\mathbf{x}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{\Sigma}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{\Sigma}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{N_{k}}\sum_{n=1}^{N}\gamma_{nk}(\mathbf{x}_{n}-\bm{\mu}_{k})(\mathbf{x}_{n}-\bm{\mu}_{k})^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{N_{k}}\sum_{n=1}^{N}\gamma_{nk}(\mathbf{x}_{n}-\bm{\mu}_{k})(\mathbf{x}_{n}-\bm{\mu}_{k})^{T}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We initialize the EM algorithm by setting local GMM means to KMeans centroids and estimate covariances and mixture weights from the dataset split induced by the KMeans cluster assignments. To ensure numerical stability during training, we regularize covariance matrices using shrinkage and diagonal loading techniques.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Graph-Regularized EM Algorithm</span></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a network (graph) <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> of nodes (clients) connected via adjacency matrix <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>. Each node <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> holds a local dataset <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{(i)}:=\{\mathbf{x}_{1}^{(i)},\dots,\mathbf{x}_{N_{i}}^{(i)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{(i)}:=\{\mathbf{x}_{1}^{(i)},\dots,\mathbf{x}_{N_{i}}^{(i)}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where each sample <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{r}^{(i)}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{r}^{(i)}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is drawn from a Gaussian mixture as in Eq. <a href="#S2.E1" title="In II Preliminaries on Finite Gaussian Mixtures ‣ Graph-Regularized Learning of Gaussian Mixture Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Node <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is connected to its neighbors <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\Gamma(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\Gamma(i)</annotation></semantics></math> via undirected edges weighted by <math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>. The graph is assumed to encode similarity among the GMM parameters of connected nodes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">GraphFed-EM training consists of <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> local EM updates (Section <a href="#S2" title="II Preliminaries on Finite Gaussian Mixtures ‣ Graph-Regularized Learning of Gaussian Mixture Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>), followed by an aggregation step. Each node <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> shares its locally estimated GMM parameters <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}^{i}=\{\bm{\mu}^{i},\bm{\Sigma}^{i},\bm{\pi}^{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝝅</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}^{i}=\{\bm{\mu}^{i},\bm{\Sigma}^{i},\bm{\pi}^{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math> with neighbors to compute a weighted average of its own and neighbors’ parameters.
Since the ordering of components is arbitrary, we first align the <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> components using the linear sum assignment solver from <a href="https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.optimize.linear_sum_assignment.html" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">SciPy</a> on a Bhattacharyya distance matrix.
The aligned parameters are aggregated as follows:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{\theta}_{aggr,k}^{i}=\frac{N_{k}^{i}\bm{\theta}_{k}^{i}+\sum_{j\in\Gamma(i)}A_{ij}N_{k}^{j}\bm{\theta}_{k}^{j}}{N_{k}^{i}+\sum_{j\in\Gamma(i)}A_{ij}N_{k}^{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{\theta}_{aggr,k}^{i}=\frac{N_{k}^{i}\bm{\theta}_{k}^{i}+\sum_{j\in\Gamma(i)}A_{ij}N_{k}^{j}\bm{\theta}_{k}^{j}}{N_{k}^{i}+\sum_{j\in\Gamma(i)}A_{ij}N_{k}^{j}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">with mixing coefficients normalized by <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k=1}^{K}N_{k}^{i}+\sum_{j\in\Gamma(i)}A_{ij}N_{k}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k=1}^{K}N_{k}^{i}+\sum_{j\in\Gamma(i)}A_{ij}N_{k}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> to ensure <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k=1}^{K}\pi_{k}^{i}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k=1}^{K}\pi_{k}^{i}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. In other words, this corresponds to a responsibility-weighted aggregation of GMM parameters.
Furthermore, the degree of parameter aggregation is controlled by <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{\theta}_{k}^{i}\leftarrow(1-\alpha)*\bm{\theta}_{k}^{i}+\alpha*\bm{\theta}_{aggr,k}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{\theta}_{k}^{i}\leftarrow(1-\alpha)*\bm{\theta}_{k}^{i}+\alpha*\bm{\theta}_{aggr,k}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">After aggregation, covariance matrices are symmetrized and diagonal-regularized to ensure positive definiteness.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Graph-Regularized EM Algorithm</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> Empirical graph <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}:=\{\mathcal{V},\mathcal{E}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}:=\{\mathcal{V},\mathcal{E}\}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> nodes and adjacency matrix <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>. Local dataset <math id="alg1.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> on each node, <math id="alg1.l1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{D}^{(i)}|=N_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{D}^{(i)}|=N_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> , number of components <math id="alg1.l1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, iterations <math id="alg1.l1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, local iterations <math id="alg1.l1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, aggregation strength <math id="alg1.l1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Initialize:</span> GMM parameters <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}^{(i,0)}=(\bm{\pi}^{(i,0)},\bm{\mu}^{(i,0)},\bm{\Sigma}^{(i,0)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝝅</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝝁</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝚺</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}^{(i,0)}=(\bm{\pi}^{(i,0)},\bm{\mu}^{(i,0)},\bm{\Sigma}^{(i,0)})</annotation></semantics></math> for each node <math id="alg1.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg1.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
            
            
            <span id="alg1.l4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> each node <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
            
            
             <span id="alg1.l5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}=1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg1.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
            
            
               Compute <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}^{(i,t_{i})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}^{(i,t_{i})}</annotation></semantics></math> with local EM updates

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
            
            
             <span id="alg1.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
            
            
             Share local estimates <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}^{(i,T_{i})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}^{(i,T_{i})}</annotation></semantics></math> with neighbors <math id="alg1.l8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(i)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
            
            
            <span id="alg1.l9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
            
            
            <span id="alg1.l10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> each node <math id="alg1.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
            
            
             Update <math id="alg1.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}^{(i,T_{i})}\leftarrow\bm{\theta}^{(i,T_{i})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}^{(i,T_{i})}\leftarrow\bm{\theta}^{(i,T_{i})}</annotation></semantics></math> via <a href="#S3.E3" title="In III Graph-Regularized EM Algorithm ‣ Graph-Regularized Learning of Gaussian Mixture Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
            
            
            <span id="alg1.l12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">13:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l13.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l14.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">14:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg1.l14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\bm{\theta}^{(i,T)}\}_{i\in\mathcal{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\bm{\theta}^{(i,T)}\}_{i\in\mathcal{V}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Related Work</span></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our GraphFed-EM algorithm is an instance of the regularized EM (see Section <a href="#S6" title="VI Graph-based aggregation as regularized EM ‣ Graph-Regularized Learning of Gaussian Mixture Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI</span></a>). Regularization in EM has been used to improve stability and convergence in low-sample regimes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, and to simplify mixture models with redundant components <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">With growing data volumes and device constraints, distributed EM has become necessary. Most approaches adopt a client–server setup to train and share a single global model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, typically assuming i.i.d. data. Some works address data heterogeneity: for example, FedGenGMM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> aggregates client-trained GMMs by re-weighting components, generating synthetic data, training a global model, and redistributing it. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> addresses the estimation of shared global GMM parameters, while introducing communication-efficient EM for federated learning with non-i.i.d. data and partial participation. Closely related to our approach, FedGrEM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> employs a gradient-based EM algorithm with a regularization term that penalizes deviations from the central aggregate parameters.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Numerical Experiments</span></h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate GraphFed-EM on synthetic and MNIST datasets modeling common client heterogeneity. The synthetic datasets include: (i) clustered GMMs with parameters <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\bm{\theta}^{(c)}\}_{c=1}^{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\bm{\theta}^{(c)}\}_{c=1}^{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, where datasets sharing the same parameters form a cluster <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}^{(c)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}^{(c)}</annotation></semantics></math>, and (ii) a shared mixture with node-specific mixing coefficients <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\pi}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝝅</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\pi}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. MNIST is used to create skewed label distributions across clients.
Performance is evaluated on local validation sets (sample size <math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}^{(val)}=500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>500</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}^{(val)}=500</annotation></semantics></math>) by average log-likelihood and normalized mutual information (NMI) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> to quantify clustering accuracy. We also report centroid estimation error:</p>
<table id="S5.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mu_{err}=\frac{1}{NK}\sum_{i\in\mathcal{V}}\sum_{k=1}^{K}\left\lVert\bm{\mu}_{k}^{c_{i}}-\bm{\mu}_{k}^{i}\right\rVert_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover></mstyle><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>k</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mu_{err}=\frac{1}{NK}\sum_{i\in\mathcal{V}}\sum_{k=1}^{K}\left\lVert\bm{\mu}_{k}^{c_{i}}-\bm{\mu}_{k}^{i}\right\rVert_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mu}_{k}^{c_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>k</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mu}_{k}^{c_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the true cluster centroid, used to generate the data for node <math id="S5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. Components are matched using the Bhattacharyya distance (Section <a href="#S3" title="III Graph-Regularized EM Algorithm ‣ Graph-Regularized Learning of Gaussian Mixture Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">All experiments are repeated 10 times, with mean and standard errors shown as y-error bars or shaded regions. Unless stated otherwise, GraphFed-EM uses aggregation strength <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=10</annotation></semantics></math> iterations, and <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}=5</annotation></semantics></math> local EM steps.
Code for these and additional experiments can be found on <a href="https://github.com/shamPJ/Graph-GMM" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">GitHub</a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">V-A</span> </span><span id="S5.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Synthetic Dataset - Clustered setting</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We test GraphFed-EM on a synthetic dataset represented by a graph <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> nodes partitioned into <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> equal-sized clusters <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathcal{C}^{(c)}\}_{c=1}^{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathcal{C}^{(c)}\}_{c=1}^{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, where node <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to a cluster <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}^{(c_{i})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}^{(c_{i})}</annotation></semantics></math>. Edges are independent Bernoulli variables <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{ij}\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{ij}\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, present with probability <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{in}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{in}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> share a cluster and <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{out}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{out}</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise; edges are unweighted, so <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{ij}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{ij}=1</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{ij}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{ij}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Each node <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> holds local data <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{(i)}=\{\mathbf{x}^{(i,1)},\dots,\mathbf{x}^{(i,N_{i})}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{(i)}=\{\mathbf{x}^{(i,1)},\dots,\mathbf{x}^{(i,N_{i})}\}</annotation></semantics></math> sampled from a <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-component GMM with parameters <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mu_{k}^{(c_{i})},\Sigma_{k}^{(c_{i})},\pi_{k}^{(c_{i})})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mu_{k}^{(c_{i})},\Sigma_{k}^{(c_{i})},\pi_{k}^{(c_{i})})</annotation></semantics></math> shared among nodes in the same cluster. To introduce heterogeneity, the means and covariance matrices undergo random rotation and shear transformations applied consistently across clusters.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">When does pooling local datasets make sense?</span>
We compare local training (each local dataset independently), centralized training (all datasets combined), and GraphFed-EM with either direct cluster-wise pooling or an aggregation step (Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ III Graph-Regularized EM Algorithm ‣ Graph-Regularized Learning of Gaussian Mixture Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>); the latter two, given true cluster identities, are termed Oracles. To isolate the benefit of aggregation over pooling, we also trained GraphFed-EM on cluster-wise pooled datasets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Experiments used <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=5</annotation></semantics></math> clusters with <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=25</annotation></semantics></math> nodes (5 per cluster), <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=3</annotation></semantics></math> components, feature dimension <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\in\{2,6,10\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\in\{2,6,10\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and local training sizes <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}\in\{10,50,100\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>50</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}\in\{10,50,100\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The connectivity matrix was set with <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{in}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{in}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{out}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{out}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Validation sets of size <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}^{(val)}=500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>500</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}^{(val)}=500</annotation></semantics></math> were stratified by cluster labels.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Results (Fig. <a href="#S5.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ V-A Synthetic Dataset - Clustered setting ‣ V Numerical Experiments ‣ Graph-Regularized Learning of Gaussian Mixture Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) show discrepancies across metrics. Centralized EM matches Oracle log-likelihoods for <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=2,6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=2,6</annotation></semantics></math>, but yields consistently lower NMI, likely due to overlap between cluster GMMs in low dimensions. Local training underperforms at <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}=10</annotation></semantics></math> but approaches GraphFed-EM as <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> grows. Parameter error <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{err}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{err}</annotation></semantics></math> is smallest for Oracles, whether pooling or aggregating.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, collaboration is most beneficial when <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is high and <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is small. Direct pooling and GraphFed-EM with <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1</annotation></semantics></math> perform similar, except at <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}=10</annotation></semantics></math>, where pooling yields higher log-likelihood but no clear advantage in NMI or <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{err}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{err}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F1.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.13855v1/d_Ni_LL.svg" id="S5.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/124;" width="476" height="124"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F1.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.13855v1/d_Ni_NMI.svg" id="S5.F1.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/107;" width="476" height="107"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F1.fig3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.13855v1/d_Ni_mu_err.svg" id="S5.F1.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/170;" width="476" height="170"></object>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 1</span>: </span><span id="S5.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison of methods across different dimensions. Local, centralized and GraphFed-EM training with local datasets pooled cluster-wise directly or indirectly via aggregation step. <math id="S5.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=5</annotation></semantics></math> cluster with <math id="S5.F1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=25</annotation></semantics></math> nodes, and <math id="S5.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=3,p_{in}=1,p_{out}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=3,p_{in}=1,p_{out}=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Cluster centroids consensus.</span>
Next, we examined how aggregation strength <math id="S5.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and connectivity of the graph affect consensus of the cluster centroids. We compute the weighted squared norm of the difference between the estimated mean vector <math id="S5.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mu}_{k}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mu}_{k}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and the consensus of neighboring nodes <math id="S5.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mu}_{k}^{i,cons}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mu}_{k}^{i,cons}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S5.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{err}^{cons}=\frac{1}{NN_{i}}\sum_{i\in\mathcal{V}}\sum_{k=1}^{K}N_{k}^{i}\left\lVert\bm{\mu}_{k}^{i}-\bm{\mu}_{k}^{i,cons}\right\rVert_{2}^{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{err}^{cons}=\frac{1}{NN_{i}}\sum_{i\in\mathcal{V}}\sum_{k=1}^{K}N_{k}^{i}\left\lVert\bm{\mu}_{k}^{i}-\bm{\mu}_{k}^{i,cons}\right\rVert_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">where</p>
<table id="S5.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mu}_{k}^{i,cons}=\frac{\sum_{j\in\Gamma(i)}A_{ij}N_{k}^{j}\bm{\mu}_{k}^{j}}{\sum_{j\in\Gamma(i)}A_{ij}N_{k}^{j}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mu}_{k}^{i,cons}=\frac{\sum_{j\in\Gamma(i)}A_{ij}N_{k}^{j}\bm{\mu}_{k}^{j}}{\sum_{j\in\Gamma(i)}A_{ij}N_{k}^{j}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S5.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ V-A Synthetic Dataset - Clustered setting ‣ V Numerical Experiments ‣ Graph-Regularized Learning of Gaussian Mixture Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows that increasing connectivity probability <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{in}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{in}</annotation></semantics></math> and aggregation strength <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> leads to smaller consensus error <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{err}^{cons}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{err}^{cons}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.13855v1/consensus.svg" id="S5.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/187;" width="476" height="187"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 2</span>: </span><span id="S5.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">GraphFed-EM consensus error on one cluster with <math id="S5.F2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=10</annotation></semantics></math> nodes, and <math id="S5.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}=10,d=10,K=3,T=50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}=10,d=10,K=3,T=50</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Robustness of GraphFed-EM to out-of-cluster connections.</span>
So far, we assumed the ”true” connectivity matrix, where only nodes in the same cluster are connected. Next, we investigate how spurious connections between different clusters affect performance in a low sample support setting.
We test out-of-cluster connectivity probabilities <math id="S5.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{out}\in\{0,0.2,0.4\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.4</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{out}\in\{0,0.2,0.4\}</annotation></semantics></math> and varying aggregation strength <math id="S5.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. The number of components is set to <math id="S5.SS1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=3</annotation></semantics></math>, local sample size <math id="S5.SS1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}=10</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=10</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS1.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{in}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{in}=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Fig. <a href="#S5.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ V-A Synthetic Dataset - Clustered setting ‣ V Numerical Experiments ‣ Graph-Regularized Learning of Gaussian Mixture Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, when <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{out}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{out}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, increasing <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> improves NMI. For <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{out}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{out}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, however, larger <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> leads to performance degradation. In these cases, an intermediate aggregation strength achieves the best results, matching Oracle performance (GraphFed-EM with the true connectivity matrix). Specifically, <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.4</annotation></semantics></math> is optimal for <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{out}\in{0.2,0.4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0.4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{out}\in{0.2,0.4}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.13855v1/alpha_NMI.svg" id="S5.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/180;" width="476" height="180"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 3</span>: </span><span id="S5.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Effect of out-of-cluster connections <math id="S5.F3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{out}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{out}</annotation></semantics></math> and agggregation strength <math id="S5.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> on performance of GraphFed-EM.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">V-B</span> </span><span id="S5.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Synthetic Dataset - client-specific priors <math id="S5.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{k}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{k}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math></span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use a dataset with shared GMM parameters but skewed feature distributions due to different mixing coefficients <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{k}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{k}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Unlike <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, we assume that this heterogeneity remains fixed during inference, and generate the validation set using the same client-specific priors <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{k}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{k}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as for training.
We define the adjacency matrix <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> by measuring the overlap between distributions:</p>
<table id="S5.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A_{ij}=\frac{\sum_{k=1}^{K}\min(\pi_{k}^{i},\pi_{k}^{j})}{\sum_{j\in\Gamma(i)}\sum_{k=1}^{K}\min(\pi_{k}^{i},\pi_{k}^{j})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A_{ij}=\frac{\sum_{k=1}^{K}\min(\pi_{k}^{i},\pi_{k}^{j})}{\sum_{j\in\Gamma(i)}\sum_{k=1}^{K}\min(\pi_{k}^{i},\pi_{k}^{j})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We can see from Figure <a href="#S5.F4.sf1" title="In Fig. 4 ‣ V-C MNIST - skewed label distribution ‣ V Numerical Experiments ‣ Graph-Regularized Learning of Gaussian Mixture Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(a)</span></a> that the centralized model fits the data best with higher dimensionality <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=6,10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=6,10</annotation></semantics></math>. GraphFed-EM outperforms local, but not centralized training. Thus, in the case of client-specific priors the centralized solution is optimal for local datasets with small sample size <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}=10</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">V-C</span> </span><span id="S5.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">MNIST - skewed label distribution</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We construct a similar dataset with client-specific priors <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{k}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{k}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> using MNIST. The dataset consists of 28x28 grayscale digit images (0 to 9). We first apply UMAP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> for dimensionality reduction to <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=\{2,6,10\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=\{2,6,10\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and set <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=10</annotation></semantics></math>, assuming one component per digit. Data are distributed across <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=10</annotation></semantics></math> nodes with skewed lable proportions using a Dirichlet distribution (concentration parameter <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.3</annotation></semantics></math>). Local sample sizes are <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}=\{50,100,200\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>50,100,200</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}=\{50,100,200\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The similarity graph is built as in Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="V-B Synthetic Dataset - client-specific priors π_k^i ‣ V Numerical Experiments ‣ Graph-Regularized Learning of Gaussian Mixture Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V-B</span></a>, based on label overlap between nodes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F4.sf2" title="In Fig. 4 ‣ V-C MNIST - skewed label distribution ‣ V Numerical Experiments ‣ Graph-Regularized Learning of Gaussian Mixture Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(b)</span></a>, GraphFed-EM achieves higher NMI values than both local and centralized GMMs across all <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d,N_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d,N_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> pairs.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F4.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.13855v1/het_NMI.svg" id="S5.F4.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/186;" width="476" height="186"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F4.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S5.F4.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Performance of GraphFed-EM on dataset with client-specific priors <math id="S5.F4.sf1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F4.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.13855v1/MNIST_NMI.svg" id="S5.F4.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/186;" width="476" height="186"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F4.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S5.F4.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Performance of GraphFed-EM on MNIST dataset with skewed label distribution. <math id="S5.F4.sf2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=10,K=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=10,K=10</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.F4.sf2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=10</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 4</span>: </span><span id="S5.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Performance of GraphFed-EM on dataset with client-specific priors and MNIST dataset with skewed label distribution.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VI </span><span id="S6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Graph-based aggregation as regularized EM</span></h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{k}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{k}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denote a generic parameter of component <math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> at node <math id="S6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., <math id="S6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mu}_{k}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mu}_{k}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma}_{k}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma}_{k}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, or <math id="S6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{k}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{k}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>), and let
<math id="S6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(i)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of neighbors of node <math id="S6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> in the empirical graph.
Let <math id="S6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{k}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{k}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be the responsibility-weighted sample count for component <math id="S6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> at node <math id="S6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. For simplicity, we assume that the GMM components of connected nodes are perfectly matched.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We can view GraphFed-EM as an instance of regularized EM, where the local EM objective is augmented with a smoothness penalty across the graph:</p>
<table id="S6.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{i}(\theta_{k}^{i})+\frac{\lambda}{2}\sum_{j\in\Gamma(i)}A_{ij}N_{k}^{j}\,\big\|\theta_{k}^{i}-\theta_{k}^{j}\big\|_{2}^{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{i}(\theta_{k}^{i})+\frac{\lambda}{2}\sum_{j\in\Gamma(i)}A_{ij}N_{k}^{j}\,\big\|\theta_{k}^{i}-\theta_{k}^{j}\big\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where the first term is the local negative <math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>-function (an upper bound on the log-likelihood <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>), <math id="S6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{i}(\theta_{k}^{i})=-Q_{i}(\theta_{k}^{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{i}(\theta_{k}^{i})=-Q_{i}(\theta_{k}^{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, and the second term penalizes dissimilarity between neighboring nodes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{i,\mathrm{EM}}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>EM</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{i,\mathrm{EM}}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is the unpenalized EM maximizer of <math id="S6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{i}(\theta_{k}^{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{i}(\theta_{k}^{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, then
<math id="S6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\ell_{i}(\theta^{i,\mathrm{EM}}_{k})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>EM</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\ell_{i}(\theta^{i,\mathrm{EM}}_{k})=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
A proximal gradient step with respect to penalty term at <math id="S6.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{i,\mathrm{EM}}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>EM</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{i,\mathrm{EM}}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is then:</p>
<table id="S6.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\theta_{k}^{i,\mathrm{new}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>new</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\theta_{k}^{i,\mathrm{new}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\theta_{k}^{i,\mathrm{EM}}-\eta\lambda\sum_{j\in\Gamma(i)}A_{ij}N_{k}^{j}\big(\theta_{k}^{i,\mathrm{EM}}-\theta_{k}^{j}\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>EM</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>EM</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\theta_{k}^{i,\mathrm{EM}}-\eta\lambda\sum_{j\in\Gamma(i)}A_{ij}N_{k}^{j}\big(\theta_{k}^{i,\mathrm{EM}}-\theta_{k}^{j}\big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=(1-\alpha)\,\theta_{k}^{i,\mathrm{EM}}+\alpha\frac{N_{k}^{i}\theta_{k}^{i,\mathrm{EM}}+\sum_{j}A_{ij}N_{k}^{j}\theta_{k}^{j}}{N_{k}^{i}+\sum_{j}A_{ij}N_{k}^{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>EM</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>EM</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=(1-\alpha)\,\theta_{k}^{i,\mathrm{EM}}+\alpha\frac{N_{k}^{i}\theta_{k}^{i,\mathrm{EM}}+\sum_{j}A_{ij}N_{k}^{j}\theta_{k}^{j}}{N_{k}^{i}+\sum_{j}A_{ij}N_{k}^{j}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p3.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S6.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\eta\lambda(N_{k}^{i}+\sum_{j}A_{ij}N_{k}^{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\eta\lambda(N_{k}^{i}+\sum_{j}A_{ij}N_{k}^{j})</annotation></semantics></math>. This is equivalent to aggregation step Eq. <a href="#S3.E3" title="In III Graph-Regularized EM Algorithm ‣ Graph-Regularized Learning of Gaussian Mixture Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
It shows that the graph-based aggregation step in GraphFed-EM can be interpreted as a proximal update on a regularized EM objective: each node updates its parameter toward a weighted average of its neighbors’ parameters, with weights given by the effective sample sizes <math id="S6.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{k}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{k}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and edge weight <math id="S6.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, the aggregation balances local evidence (from node <math id="S6.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>’s own data) with graph-based smoothing (information from neighbors), which provides a connection between GraphFed-EM and regularized EM.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VII </span><span id="S7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Conclusions</span></h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce a simple decentralized graph-regularized federated learning algorithm for Gaussian mixture models. Our approach is tailored for peer-to-peer collaborative learning and is particularly effective in heterogeneous client environments. Unlike centralized solutions, it leverages graph-based regularization to promote information sharing among related clients while respecting local data characteristics.
Numerical experiments show that GraphFed-EM effectively balances the trade-off between achieving consensus among connected nodes and optimizing local objectives. In particular, it outperforms both local and centralized training in low-sample regimes, where the data dimensionality is comparable to or larger than the number of local samples.
Our method does not require full participation of all clients and can be adapted to client-specific communication and computational constraints. Future extensions include dynamically inferring the similarity graph during communication rounds, in the spirit of clustered FL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, and analyzing the connectivity conditions necessary to guarantee recovery of local GMM parameters and algorithmic convergence.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VIII </span><span id="S8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Compliance with Ethical Standards</span></h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This is a numerical simulation study for which no ethical approval was required.</p>
</div>
</section>
